<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Old Calabar</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1884 CE (approx.) · capital Duke Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Efik trading towns on the Calabar river, governed by the Ekpe society and major ports of the slave and palm oil trades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/efik">Efik</a> settled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabar">Calabar</a> river in the 17th century, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creek-town">Creek Town</a>, Old Town and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-town">Duke Town</a>, which Europeans called Old Calabar. They became middlemen between European ships and the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river">Cross river</a> hinterland, from whom they bought slaves.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>There was no single king; the towns were ruled by heads of trading houses and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekpe">Ekpe</a>, the leopard society, which made laws, enforced debts and bound European traders. The diary of the trader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antera-duke">Antera Duke</a> from the 1780s records life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-town">Duke Town</a>. After abolition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> replaced slaves, and Scottish Presbyterian missionaries arrived in 1846.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/efik">Efik</a> chiefs signed a British protection treaty in 1884, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabar">Calabar</a> became the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oil-rivers-protectorate">Oil Rivers Protectorate</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/niger-coast-protectorate">Niger Coast Protectorate</a>. It is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river-state">Cross River State</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obong-of-calabar">Obong of Calabar</a> reigns.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/old-calabar.jpg" alt="Old Calabar" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: Henry Morton Stanley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanley_Founding_of_Congo_Free_State_232_Old_Calabar_Factories_near_Duke_Town.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Duke Town</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duke Ephraim, Eyo Honesty II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Efik traditional religion and Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Efik</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Calabar, Efik city-states, Akwa Akpa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1742&amp;focus=old-calabar" aria-label="Old Calabar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4911 1992 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Old Calabar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5231 2248L5175 2243"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5175 2243h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5231 2248h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5231 2248h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1742&amp;focus=old-calabar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

